    {
      "path": ".claude/scripts/ocr-usage-wait.sh",
      "content": "On Linux (GNU mktemp), `mktemp -d -t ocr-probe` fails because the template lacks trailing `X` characters. Since the failure is not checked, `dir` is empty and the review writes to `/p.json`, typically causing a permissions failure instead of probing the provider. Use a portable template and stop if directory creation fails, e.g. `dir=$(mktemp -d \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/ocr-probe.XXXXXX\") || return 1`.",
      "suggestion_code": "  dir=$(mktemp -d \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/ocr-probe.XXXXXX\") || return 1",
      "existing_code": "  dir=$(mktemp -d -t ocr-probe)",
      "start_line": 13,
      "end_line": 13,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": ".claude/scripts/ocr-usage-wait.sh",
      "content": "This treats every provider failure as an exhausted usage quota. Authentication failures, an unavailable model, and provider/network errors can also be provider failures, so these now return 2 and make the caller retry for up to six hours instead of stopping and reporting the actionable error. Check for the specific usage-limit error (such as `subscription_sharing_usage_limit_exceeded`) before returning 2; return 1 with the diagnostic for other provider failures.",
      "existing_code": "  if jq -e '[.. | objects | select(.classification? == \"provider\")] | length \u003e 0' \"$dir/p.json\" \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261; then\n    echo \"$(TZ=America/New_York date +%H:%M) still limited (provider failure)\"\n    return 2",
      "start_line": 20,
      "end_line": 22,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "src/ui/screens/MarksStep.tsx",
